# Accepted findings, each with the reason it is accepted and what would change
# that. Nothing is listed here to make a report look clean; a finding that is
# actually reachable gets fixed instead.
#
# Review this file whenever a base image or a dependency is bumped: an
# acceptance that was sound at one version is not automatically sound at the
# next.

# --- pip's vendored libraries -------------------------------------------------
#
# Both of these are inside pip's own `_vendor` tree in the research image. They
# are not application dependencies, are not importable by the application, and
# are only reachable while pip itself is running -- which the service never does
# at runtime. They are present in the CURRENT pip (26.2.1), so there is no
# newer pip that resolves them.
#
# What would change this: pip re-vendoring newer copies, at which point these
# lines should be removed and the finding should simply disappear.
#
# msgpack 1.1.2 -> 1.2.1, in pip/_vendor
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964
# setuptools 70.3.0 -> 78.1.1, recorded in pip/_vendor/vendor.txt
CVE-2025-47273
# setuptools 70.3.0 -> 83.0.0. The same phantom package as the line above: a
# NEW advisory against the same version string, so it arrives through the same
# route and is accepted for the same reason.
CVE-2026-59890

# Re-verified on 2026-09-14 against a `docker build --no-cache --pull` of the
# research image, rather than taken on trust from the previous review:
#
#   * The IMPORTABLE setuptools is 84.0.0
#     (/opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/setuptools/__init__.py), which is
#     above both fixed versions. The only setuptools dist-info on disk is
#     84.0.0, in both site-packages trees.
#   * There is NO setuptools 70.3.0 anywhere in the image. The version Trivy
#     reports comes from the line `setuptools==70.3.0` in
#     pip/_vendor/vendor.txt -- a text record of what pip vendored, not
#     installed code. Nothing can import it because it is not there.
#   * msgpack 1.1.2 exists only as pip/_vendor/msgpack, pip's own copy.
#
# So these are findings against a package the image does not actually contain
# at that version, and the fix is already applied for the one that is real.
# Raw totals from that build: 150 OS findings (0 CRITICAL, 44 HIGH, 48 MEDIUM,
# 57 LOW, 1 UNKNOWN) plus 3 Python findings; CI-gated (CRITICAL/HIGH,
# --ignore-unfixed, this file applied): 0.

# --- golang.org/x/crypto/openpgp ---------------------------------------------
#
# GO-2026-5932: the openpgp package in golang.org/x/crypto is unmaintained and
# unsafe by design. There is no fixed version and there will not be one.
#
# The control plane requires golang.org/x/crypto for Argon2id password hashing
# and imports nothing else from the module. govulncheck confirms the symbol is
# unreachable: "Your code is affected by 0 vulnerabilities."
#
# What would change this: any code in this repository importing
# golang.org/x/crypto/openpgp, which it must not.
GO-2026-5932

# --- The research image's Debian base, and why nothing is listed for it ------
#
# Scanned WITHOUT --ignore-unfixed on 2026-09-08, the research image reports 54
# HIGH/CRITICAL findings from its base (python:3.12.12-slim-trixie, Debian
# 13.6): perl-base (3 CRITICAL), util-linux and its libraries, ncurses, gzip,
# libacl1, libsqlite3-0, libsystemd0. Re-scanned the same day after the
# reconciliation work: 173 findings at every severity (3 CRITICAL, 51 HIGH, 57
# MEDIUM, 57 LOW, 5 UNKNOWN) from the base, unchanged. The control-api image
# reports 1 (the openpgp module note below) and the web image reports none at
# any severity.
#
# Every one has an empty fixed-version field: Debian has not published a fix.
# The Dockerfile already runs `apt-get upgrade -y`, so there is nothing to
# apply, and CI passes only because it uses --ignore-unfixed. They are NOT
# listed in this file, because listing them would suppress the same CVE ids
# once fixes DO land -- which is exactly when they should reappear.
#
# What bounds the exposure: the research service holds no broker client and no
# credentials pointing back at the control plane, its database role is
# read-only, it runs non-root on a read-only root filesystem, and none of the
# affected packages (perl, mount helpers, ncurses) are executed by uvicorn or
# the numeric stack.
#
# What would actually resolve it: a minimal base. The image is 813 MB with 87
# Debian packages, of which the service executes a handful.
#
# Distroless was ATTEMPTED in the intelligence milestone and deliberately not
# adopted. The measurement, so the next attempt starts from it rather than
# repeating the work:
#
#   * scipy and scikit-learn bundle their own libgomp and OpenBLAS inside their
#     wheels (scipy.libs/, scikit_learn.libs/), so no system libgomp is needed
#     -- which was the assumption that made this look hard.
#   * What IS needed is 29 system libraries, discovered by running ldd over
#     every extension module and over lib-dynload: libc, libm, libstdc++,
#     libgcc_s, libz, liblzma, libffi, libuuid, libcrypt, libcrypto, libssl,
#     libsqlite3, libncursesw, libtinfo, libpanelw, libreadline, libgdbm,
#     libdb-5.3, libnsl, libtirpc, libresolv, libgssapi_krb5, libkrb5,
#     libk5crypto, libkrb5support, libcom_err, libkeyutils, libdl, libpthread.
#
# The reason it was not adopted is the important part. Perl, apt, dpkg, gzip,
# util-linux and coreutils would genuinely be REMOVED. But sqlite3, ncurses,
# krb5 and openssl would be COPIED IN AS BARE FILES, with no package database
# behind them -- so Trivy would stop reporting their CVEs while the vulnerable
# code was still in the image. The scan would go from 173 findings to nearly
# zero, and almost none of that improvement would be real.
#
# Reporting that as a security win would be exactly the kind of number-chasing
# this file exists to prevent. The honest options, in order of preference:
#
#   1. Trim what Python's stdlib links against, by not shipping the lib-dynload
#      modules the service never imports (readline, sqlite3, dbm, nis, curses).
#      That removes the libraries rather than hiding them.
#   2. A distroless variant that ships a package inventory a scanner can read.
#   3. Wait for Debian to publish fixes, which is what --ignore-unfixed is
#      already doing.
#
# Option 1 is the recommended next step and is recorded as remaining work.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# urllib3 2.7.0, two HIGH findings in the quant image. Suppressed with the
# evidence below, and ONLY these two ids.
#
#   CVE-2026-97687  traffic interception via HTTPS proxy TLS config override
#   CVE-2026-97689  denial of service via unbounded allocation in the chunk
#                   parser
#
# Why not fixed: there is nothing here to upgrade.
#
# urllib3 is not a dependency of this service. It does not appear in
# pyproject.toml, it is not installed as a package, and it exists in the image
# at exactly two paths, both inside pip's own vendor directory:
#
#   /opt/venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/pip/_vendor/urllib3
#   /usr/local/lib/python3.12/site-packages/pip/_vendor/urllib3
#
# Verified in the built image rather than assumed:
#
#   $ docker run --rm --entrypoint sh <image> -c "python -c 'import urllib3'"
#   ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'urllib3'
#
# It is not importable. The research service cannot execute it under any code
# path. The only program that can is pip, and pip runs at image BUILD time, not
# at runtime -- the final stage does not install packages.
#
# Why not upgrade pip instead: the Dockerfile already does
# `pip install --upgrade pip`, the image carries **pip 26.2.1**, and
# `pip index versions pip` confirms that is the newest release in existence.
# It still vendors urllib3==2.7.0 (pip/_vendor/vendor.txt). There is no pip to
# move to.
#
# What would change this: a pip release vendoring urllib3 >= 2.8.0. At that
# point the existing `pip install --upgrade pip` picks it up with no change
# here, and these two lines should be deleted. Check on the next image rebuild
# that reports them as fixed.
#
# This is the same shape as the setuptools 70.3.0 finding recorded above: a
# scanner reading vendored metadata as though it were installed code. It is
# recorded separately rather than folded in, because the next person needs to
# be able to delete one without reasoning about the other.
CVE-2026-97687
CVE-2026-97689
